1
00:05:19,930 --> 00:06:15,340
Thank you, Dale. Ladies and gentlemen, let me first say that I am exceedingly happy to

2
00:06:15,340 --> 00:06:21,940
be back here again. We moved out a little over ten years ago, as Dale said, to a warmer

3
00:06:21,940 --> 00:06:28,280
climate. We actually moved to relax, to take it a little easier, as a result of which both

4
00:06:28,280 --> 00:06:33,420
my wife and I worked three times as hard as we ever did before, which I don't think I

5
00:06:33,420 --> 00:06:43,630
to explain to you the way. But this is still home and particularly this building and the

6
00:06:43,630 --> 00:06:50,840
people here and after more than 20 years on the faculty it really is the only home a vagrant

7
00:06:50,840 --> 00:06:57,800
like myself has and so I'm very grateful to be allowed back and very happy to be here

8
00:06:57,800 --> 00:07:04,960
and very happy to see so many veterans around here, but also so many newcomers.

9
00:07:06,440 --> 00:07:11,520
Before we started, Madeleine Stanley, who, as some of you know,

10
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:16,360
is responsible for all the things that go right in these meetings,

11
00:07:16,700 --> 00:07:19,320
and I'm responsible for all the things that go wrong.

12
00:07:20,560 --> 00:07:25,680
Madeleine told me that this is our 10th meeting in this format,

13
00:07:25,680 --> 00:07:32,040
although we began them in 1950 as a fact series of faculty lectures.

14
00:07:32,920 --> 00:07:37,620
And so I feel that this is a particularly happy occasion,

15
00:07:37,820 --> 00:07:44,100
both the 10th and the 30th anniversary, I think, of these series.

16
00:07:46,600 --> 00:07:54,380
You know, I don't know when we first talked about productivity in the symposia,

17
00:07:54,380 --> 00:07:57,800
but my memory says about 80 years ago,

18
00:07:57,800 --> 00:08:04,410
And at that time, that was a new topic, and nobody else paid much attention.

19
00:08:05,750 --> 00:08:11,350
And some of you kind of said, why should we pay attention?

20
00:08:11,510 --> 00:08:14,050
It's always been taking care of itself.

21
00:08:14,870 --> 00:08:23,760
Which, by the way, was the answer that made me not become an economist 50 years ago.

22
00:08:24,420 --> 00:08:29,820
I was the youngest member of the Keynes Seminar in Cambridge, invited members only.

23
00:08:29,820 --> 00:08:35,270
and I was very dubious about where I was going to go

24
00:08:35,270 --> 00:08:38,030
and economics was a possibility

25
00:08:38,030 --> 00:08:41,230
and a strong attraction

26
00:08:41,230 --> 00:08:46,710
until one of the people in there

27
00:08:46,710 --> 00:08:49,760
I think quite sure it was Abba Lerner

28
00:08:49,760 --> 00:08:52,640
asked the master what about productivity

29
00:08:52,640 --> 00:08:54,720
why isn't it in your system

30
00:08:54,720 --> 00:09:00,670
and the master said we don't have to put it in

31
00:09:00,670 --> 00:09:02,570
it takes care of itself

32
00:09:02,570 --> 00:09:09,360
provided only that there is full employee confidence. That was the answer

33
00:09:09,360 --> 00:09:18,930
that I think made me decide that any discipline that so avaliably can

34
00:09:18,930 --> 00:09:24,210
dismiss things one doesn't understand is not for me. I've always been more

35
00:09:24,210 --> 00:09:31,000
interested in the things one can't understand than in the ones one can.

36
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:35,980
But eight years ago this was still a prevalent view. It takes somehow care of

37
00:09:35,980 --> 00:09:42,740
of itself. Now we know it doesn't. Or if it does, like all things, they take care of themselves,

38
00:09:42,740 --> 00:09:49,880
it goes down and not up. And we have learned a great deal in the last eight years, and

39
00:09:49,880 --> 00:09:55,040
the first thing we have learned, and it's the most important one, is the one Dean Mays

40
00:09:55,040 --> 00:10:02,360
mentioned in his introduction. The decline in productivity is not an American phenomenon.

41
00:10:02,360 --> 00:10:11,740
It is a worldwide phenomenon of all developed and incidentally equally of the rapidly industrializing developing countries.

42
00:10:11,740 --> 00:10:17,040
It is greatest behind the Iron Curtain.

43
00:10:17,440 --> 00:10:22,220
Poland is where the Iron Curtain was lifted for a little bit so we get a glimpse in.

44
00:10:22,580 --> 00:10:28,100
It's only an example, but the Soviet Union is probably much more pronounced

45
00:10:28,100 --> 00:10:38,580
where it is quite clear that in the one sector, which in the developed world still has fairly rapid increases in productivity, namely agriculture,

46
00:10:39,160 --> 00:10:45,120
the Soviet Union has the extraordinary achievement of negative productivities.

47
00:10:45,980 --> 00:10:52,680
The last 20 years since the Khrushchev began to divert capital to agriculture

48
00:10:52,680 --> 00:10:58,570
and to raise wages on the farm sufficiently to hold people,

49
00:10:59,170 --> 00:11:04,680
the more capital was poured in, the lower the harvests are,

50
00:11:04,760 --> 00:11:07,100
not just proportionately, but absolutely,

51
00:11:07,320 --> 00:11:10,000
and the more labour there is on the Russian farm.

52
00:11:10,180 --> 00:11:14,260
That's negative productivity, and that is a great achievement.

53
00:11:14,260 --> 00:11:18,000
I wouldn't say unprecedented, but there are not many precedents.

54
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:23,760
And the rest of the developed world, their rate of productivity decreased.

55
00:11:23,760 --> 00:11:31,880
is about the same, though that isn't quite true. And I shock you by saying that the fastest

56
00:11:31,880 --> 00:11:38,580
rate of productivity decreases in Japan. Because you look at labor productivity only, and say

57
00:11:38,580 --> 00:11:44,420
that the rate of productivity decreases is the same as in this country or in Germany,

58
00:11:44,420 --> 00:11:52,840
not as great as in Sweden. The Japanese just started from a very high basis, so they still

59
00:11:52,840 --> 00:11:59,720
increased labor productivity, but at a decreasing rate. If you plop it on log-log paper, the

60
00:11:59,720 --> 00:12:06,030
curve is almost strictly parallel to ours. But the productivity of capital in Japan has

61
00:12:06,030 --> 00:12:13,740
been going down about twice as fast as ours. And these are very dubious measurements, but

62
00:12:13,740 --> 00:12:24,880
the magnitudes are reliable to the point where... Is it better now? Yeah. Well, will you please

63
00:12:24,880 --> 00:12:29,440
not suffer in silence and if you have any trouble will you please say louder

64
00:12:29,440 --> 00:12:36,710
because we did I put it aside because I got an echo do you get an echo what yeah

65
00:12:36,710 --> 00:12:43,290
that's what we did that's why I put it aside let me and maybe it's hearing aid

66
00:12:43,290 --> 00:12:51,870
batteries of man no it isn't so okay and in Japan if you look at the automobile

67
00:12:51,870 --> 00:12:55,690
industry let me see the automobile industry of the world sets in greatest

68
00:12:55,690 --> 00:13:04,910
trouble. It's the Japanese. It is a 5% drop in automobile sales, and Toyota and Nissan

69
00:13:04,910 --> 00:13:11,650
are both bankrupt. They have those incredibly fixed capital costs. And if you look at a

70
00:13:11,650 --> 00:13:16,810
balance sheet, and an old financial man like myself looks at balance sheets before he looks

71
00:13:16,810 --> 00:13:26,560
at P&Ls, because I know how to fake a P&L, a balance sheet is harder. In this country,

72
00:13:26,560 --> 00:13:35,260
Already the lawyers would already have drawn up the petition for Chapter 11 for them.

73
00:13:35,260 --> 00:13:40,880
The fixed capital costs are incredible, and if you look at the last ten years and relate

74
00:13:40,880 --> 00:13:51,830
the capital invested in those two companies to the output they have, as they went from

75
00:13:51,830 --> 00:13:56,910
if 1970 is 1 to 1, it's now about 2.2 to 1.

76
00:13:56,910 --> 00:14:01,670
They need more than twice the amount of capital for each additional unit of output.

77
00:14:01,670 --> 00:14:10,510
And that, as any old financial man can tell you, is the road to receivership, the fastest road to receivership.

78
00:14:11,090 --> 00:14:19,290
And so one of the reasons why they are pushing cars so hard is clearly not to maintain employment, but to maintain solvency.

79
00:14:19,290 --> 00:14:27,290
And one reason why they want us and the Europeans to lower the boom is so that they then can blame us.

80
00:14:27,290 --> 00:14:36,520
But the rate of capital productivity has been going down faster in Japan than in this country,

81
00:14:36,520 --> 00:14:39,520
though it's not doing too well here either.

82
00:14:39,520 --> 00:14:49,520
And then let me say the first thing we know is that none of the explanations for this decrease can be accepted.

83
00:14:49,520 --> 00:14:54,320
Because all of them look at national factors.

84
00:14:55,890 --> 00:15:02,430
Bob and I, Bob Hawking, talked yesterday about it, and he mentioned the shift to service employment,

85
00:15:02,930 --> 00:15:07,910
which is a very plausible explanation for what happened in this country.

86
00:15:08,370 --> 00:15:16,160
But you know, there's been no such shift in the United Kingdom, no such shift in the Soviet Union,

87
00:15:16,660 --> 00:15:19,600
and not much of such a shift in Japan.

88
00:15:19,600 --> 00:15:24,580
And yet productivities behave the same way.

89
00:15:25,400 --> 00:15:31,120
And almost anything you can say is a local phenomenon.

90
00:15:31,380 --> 00:15:37,180
And one cannot explain generic events by local phenomena.

91
00:15:37,760 --> 00:15:50,180
And so let's be very skeptical because if you go by these explanations, you are then likely to do the wrong things.

92
00:15:50,180 --> 00:16:12,320
Let me say in retrospect, we also realize that the explanations we always said for the 100 years in the rise of poverty are very deep, though they have more evidence, more backing than the phenomenon.

93
00:16:12,320 --> 00:17:01,700
And apparently, we do not understand that you may see one reason for this is that economists, in order to have a standing, it's a system. And so, such things as productivity, which are clearly related to both technology and demographics, are very hard to explain in terms of an economic model.

94
00:17:01,700 --> 00:17:34,580
But we know that we have to be very careful. That's number one. And for a while I thought all the declining productivities have in common that the government overload, the amount of the national productive proportion is distributed through governmental channel is rising to the declining productivity.

95
00:17:34,580 --> 00:17:42,890
negative correlation, except that it doesn't quite fit Japan, where the proportion of national

96
00:17:42,890 --> 00:17:49,170
product that is distributed by government has not been rising significantly, and in

97
00:17:49,170 --> 00:17:53,110
fact may have been going down up until about three years ago.

98
00:17:53,770 --> 00:18:00,530
So there is no one planation that fits the observed fact.

99
00:18:00,530 --> 00:18:13,380
And then one is very leery about the hypothesis and one says, maybe this is a factor, maybe that is a factor, but one doesn't say this is the explanation.

100
00:18:13,380 --> 00:18:30,820
The second thing we do know is that there has been a change in, we don't again, in the constellation up till make it 1870 and most of you still operate on that basis.

101
00:18:30,820 --> 00:18:53,750
One could argue that productivities of the factors of production can allow the substitutes. In other words, that you can trade more capital investment against higher labor productivity and less labor and so on, and vice versa.

102
00:18:55,070 --> 00:18:58,870
Although, let me say, the evidence for this was always shaky.

103
00:19:00,560 --> 00:19:08,880
Somebody who looks at businesses and industries the way I do, rather than at economic models, always saw exceptions.

104
00:19:08,880 --> 00:19:15,390
And now, I think it is safer to assume that it doesn't work.

105
00:19:18,280 --> 00:19:30,580
Japanese automobile industry is a good example, where the productivity of labor is, despite all the robotization you hear about,

106
00:19:30,580 --> 00:19:35,830
has not really been doing very well compared to historical reasons.

107
00:19:36,330 --> 00:19:43,230
Though, let's not go into this, because that's a whole pack full of marked cards,

108
00:19:43,230 --> 00:19:50,100
such as the age of the labor force and the relation in Japan between wage and age and so on.

109
00:19:51,200 --> 00:20:02,610
But clearly that tremendous increase in capital investment has so far not produced a rapid increase in labor productivity.

110
00:20:02,930 --> 00:20:10,490
in Japanese have become no less labor-intensive, but tremendously more capital-intensive.

111
00:20:10,490 --> 00:20:17,580
And that's true of our steel industry to a large extent, too.

112
00:20:17,580 --> 00:20:24,580
And you may say so much of our capital investment in the steel industry has not been in productive equipment,

113
00:20:24,580 --> 00:20:29,580
but in pollution control, but the fact remains that the traditional,

114
00:20:29,580 --> 00:20:35,280
call it the Kuznets relationship, because Simon Kuznets got the Nobel Prize for showing

115
00:20:35,280 --> 00:20:40,820
this, where it means capital investment and increased labor productivity can no longer

116
00:20:40,820 --> 00:20:42,140
be taken for granted.

117
00:20:44,470 --> 00:20:53,650
Except that there is also a very peculiar phenomenon, that perhaps what has happened

118
00:20:53,650 --> 00:20:59,910
is that instead of a linear curve, which Kuznets showed, you have an exponential one, which

119
00:20:59,910 --> 00:21:05,830
means that up to a very substantial point, more capital does not mean more labor productivity.

120
00:21:06,550 --> 00:21:13,730
And then when you reach total automation, it breaks through. Again, you have a nice

121
00:21:13,730 --> 00:21:26,300
example. Five years ago, the microchip manufacturers had to make a decision how to handle the very

122
00:21:26,300 --> 00:21:33,670
labor-intensive stages of the work in developed countries. Ours, let's say an Intel, made

123
00:21:33,670 --> 00:21:43,060
a decision to farm it out to where you have cheap labor until chips are assembled in Nigeria with

124
00:21:43,060 --> 00:21:51,090
very cheap labor and incidentally very very good labor in terms of productivity the Japanese are

125
00:21:51,090 --> 00:21:58,530
temperamentally adverse to entrusting anything to a foreigner who is not part of their culture

126
00:21:58,530 --> 00:22:05,510
and so they made the at that time it seemed almost asinine decision to try to automate

127
00:22:05,510 --> 00:22:12,560
something which everybody knew couldn't be automated well they succeeded and even though

128
00:22:12,560 --> 00:22:20,470
the capital investment is very high the productivity is much greater and the quality

129
00:22:20,470 --> 00:22:26,950
is much better that's why our microchip people are so afraid now of the japanese competition

130
00:22:26,950 --> 00:22:33,990
so if you can go into total automation this is near total automation then you probably get the

131
00:22:33,990 --> 00:22:40,820
the old trade-off again. And incidentally, there are now enough examples to prove, and

132
00:22:40,820 --> 00:22:49,200
I didn't see it, but now that if you can get total automation, you are infinitely more

133
00:22:49,200 --> 00:22:57,210
productive and more competitive than anything based on even the cheapest label. And if you

134
00:22:57,210 --> 00:23:05,480
have the knowledge base in your society, if you have the people who are trained and

135
00:23:05,480 --> 00:23:12,550
schooled, as the Japanese have, as we have, as the Germans have, the British do not have.

136
00:23:12,550 --> 00:23:19,830
The British have probably, of all developed countries today, the poorest knowledge base

137
00:23:19,830 --> 00:23:26,040
since for 30 years they have not developed middle management people and it's beginning to show very

138
00:23:26,040 --> 00:23:38,380
badly. They are very good at the top, in the middle they have not. Then the absence of those 30 years,

139
00:23:38,380 --> 00:23:46,660
not of the Harvard Business School, but of the North Dakota State Business School shows.

140
00:23:46,660 --> 00:23:52,920
And in the long run, believe me, the North Dakota State Business School may be more important

141
00:23:52,920 --> 00:23:58,650
than the Harvard Business School because it gives you the support people. And without

142
00:23:58,650 --> 00:24:03,950
the support people, you know, a leader is somebody who's got followers. And if you ain't

143
00:24:03,950 --> 00:24:11,590
got the followers, then having the Harvard Business School people doesn't help you. And

144
00:24:11,590 --> 00:24:16,470
That's the British situation, and that's why I think Britain is in much greater danger,

145
00:24:16,610 --> 00:24:21,760
in much worse straits than the figures indicate.

146
00:24:21,760 --> 00:24:30,540
And the old generation of middle management who are basically trained as NCOs or subalterns in World War II

147
00:24:30,540 --> 00:24:31,860
are getting old.

148
00:24:32,640 --> 00:24:37,440
The replacements aren't there and aren't really well trained by modern standards.

149
00:24:37,440 --> 00:24:47,780
So if you can automate, really automate, which puts tremendous demands on that educated,

150
00:24:47,780 --> 00:24:54,720
competent, responsible and responsive middle management structure, professional structure,

151
00:24:54,720 --> 00:24:58,720
then you are more productive, then the cheapest labour.

152
00:24:58,720 --> 00:25:03,720
So at the extreme, the old trade-off may still hold, but only at the extreme.

153
00:25:03,720 --> 00:25:07,720
And only in that direction, not on the other side. And we know that.

154
00:25:07,720 --> 00:25:21,160
And we know, therefore, that activity first is not the productivity of one factor, but of all factors.

155
00:25:22,610 --> 00:25:26,430
And there are basically, well, either three or four.

156
00:25:29,210 --> 00:25:33,940
There's money, and there are key physical resources.

157
00:25:35,750 --> 00:25:40,700
And now you expect me to say people, and I'm disappointed there is time.

158
00:25:40,700 --> 00:25:49,620
Because for the people who are critical, which are your management people, your technical people,

159
00:25:49,620 --> 00:26:02,410
the teacher in the classroom. Time is really the resource, and basically when you look at the barriers to their productivity,

160
00:26:02,830 --> 00:26:10,440
they are largely that they aren't given the time to do the things they know how to do.

161
00:26:10,900 --> 00:26:16,860
The best example of this, if you want to see it, you know, you all talk of a nurse's shortage.

162
00:26:17,060 --> 00:26:21,740
There is no such thing. They are underpaid, but there is no nurse's shortage.

163
00:26:21,740 --> 00:26:29,810
If we use the nurses we have for nursing instead of for paperwork, we'd have a nurse's glut.

164
00:26:31,050 --> 00:26:38,110
But 70% of their time today in the typical community hospital, those poor women fill out forms.

165
00:26:39,830 --> 00:26:44,450
And the time is being paid for nursing.

166
00:26:45,390 --> 00:26:47,790
Their wage scale reflects the fact.

167
00:26:47,790 --> 00:26:58,340
Therefore, they are paid on the basis of low clerks, which is what they actually use their time,

168
00:26:59,160 --> 00:27:03,120
and not on the basis of what they are being trained for, which is patient care,

169
00:27:03,360 --> 00:27:08,120
which would probably be at least twice what they are making on average, and be cheap at that.

170
00:27:08,700 --> 00:27:18,680
And so when you put in a clerk who handles the paperwork, you suddenly have a surplus, and then you can pay.

171
00:27:18,680 --> 00:27:21,420
And that is productivity of time.

172
00:27:21,420 --> 00:27:29,010
And those are really the three factors which together give you overall...

173
00:27:29,010 --> 00:27:31,010
How is the sound now, all right?

174
00:27:31,010 --> 00:27:33,480
You there in the back, can you hear?

175
00:27:33,480 --> 00:27:34,480
Thanks.

176
00:27:34,480 --> 00:27:38,480
And thanks, Frank, for getting the feedback out.

177
00:27:38,480 --> 00:27:49,830
And so we know that productivity is a combination of the three or the configuration of the three.

178
00:27:49,830 --> 00:27:53,830
And we are now beginning to talk about total productivity.

179
00:27:53,830 --> 00:27:58,590
but we are not yet really able to measure it.

180
00:27:59,450 --> 00:28:01,530
It's a concept so far.

181
00:28:01,710 --> 00:28:03,710
There's a lot of good work going on

182
00:28:03,710 --> 00:28:06,470
but it is very hard to measure it.

183
00:28:06,470 --> 00:28:09,710
But we also know that one can't work at it.

184
00:28:11,380 --> 00:28:14,540
One has to work at each resource separately.

185
00:28:17,830 --> 00:28:20,990
And the trade-off decisions have to be made

186
00:28:20,990 --> 00:28:23,650
as expedient ad hoc decisions

187
00:28:23,650 --> 00:28:25,790
because we have no theory really

188
00:28:25,790 --> 00:28:29,470
and yet it has to be done.

189
00:28:29,730 --> 00:28:35,260
And so the first thing we know is that one has to work.

190
00:28:35,520 --> 00:28:40,450
And that's an interesting thing,

191
00:28:41,160 --> 00:28:46,080
because this is almost the opposite of what Keynes and his generation believed.

192
00:28:46,080 --> 00:28:55,780
They believed that activities are a reaction to outward economic stimuli.

193
00:28:59,160 --> 00:29:00,940
And this doesn't work anymore.

194
00:29:01,100 --> 00:29:03,540
If it ever did, maybe it did.

195
00:29:06,890 --> 00:29:08,010
When you look at the history,

196
00:29:08,270 --> 00:29:08,470
it didn't.

197
00:29:15,320 --> 00:29:19,910
Dick Taylor and this kind of George Siemens of the Deutsche Bank in Europe

198
00:29:19,910 --> 00:29:34,120
were not reactions, not even economic accolados moralists who said it is the duty of a manager

199
00:29:34,120 --> 00:29:40,360
to make resources productive because they are in his keeping, not because of cost.

200
00:29:42,050 --> 00:29:44,390
In all of Taylor, there is no mention of cost.

201
00:29:45,270 --> 00:29:47,790
The word does not exist in his vocabulary.

202
00:29:48,670 --> 00:29:53,710
The word is duty, and he saw it as a moral obligation.

203
00:29:53,890 --> 00:29:55,610
So did George Siemens, the banker.

204
00:29:57,200 --> 00:30:00,820
And so these were not reactions to economic stimuli.

205
00:30:00,820 --> 00:30:05,710
There were economic rewards, but not economic motivations.

206
00:30:06,910 --> 00:30:12,050
And I think this is what the generation of 1930 did not understand.

207
00:30:13,950 --> 00:30:18,380
And because we lost that moral imperative.

208
00:30:22,900 --> 00:30:25,900
Rewards are economic, but the motivations must not be.

209
00:30:26,670 --> 00:30:30,040
The penalties are economic, but not entirely.

210
00:30:30,200 --> 00:30:31,640
They are also probably moral.

211
00:30:33,200 --> 00:30:36,860
And so we know that one has to work on productivity.

212
00:30:36,860 --> 00:31:05,790
Productivity crisis. You know, when I came in in the 20s, my first teacher of economics was a directist, the only survivor of Kondratyev's disciples. All the others were shot by Stalin. He got over the border.

213
00:31:05,790 --> 00:31:14,790
And so I started out with the Kondratiev Cycle in 1927, which was a very good time for it.

214
00:31:14,790 --> 00:31:21,700
And believe me, the evidence is again very popular, but the evidence is not convincing.

215
00:31:21,700 --> 00:31:27,700
Because if there were a Kondratiev Cycle, you should have a productivity crisis every 50 years.

216
00:31:27,700 --> 00:31:34,110
And there was. There was only one big one in the 18th century.

217
00:31:34,110 --> 00:31:46,170
the French. French mercantilism ended in a major crisis of productivity and of capital formation,

218
00:31:46,170 --> 00:31:52,490
and that's when the first scientific revolution in economics occurred. The physiocrats

219
00:31:52,490 --> 00:31:59,980
who looked across the channel and that those unsystematic English who had no theory in 1730

220
00:32:01,450 --> 00:32:09,450
until muddled through and who did everything wrong, but who had steady increase in productivity

221
00:32:09,450 --> 00:32:15,490
and a rapid rate of capital formation, whereas the one country in which economies to end

222
00:32:15,490 --> 00:32:21,370
the government, namely France, was the richest country of Europe, was rapidly going down

223
00:32:21,370 --> 00:32:22,010
the spout.

224
00:32:22,770 --> 00:32:26,370
If that sounds contemporary, it's not intentional.

225
00:32:26,950 --> 00:32:28,710
All the characters are fictitious.

226
00:32:31,500 --> 00:32:38,620
But France invented the Council of Economic Advisers in 1708, and the decline began in

227
00:32:38,620 --> 00:32:39,400
1720.

228
00:32:39,400 --> 00:33:01,240
And the lead times haven't changed much, largely for the same reason, that instead of trying to understand, they thought they understood and began to manipulate.

229
00:33:03,100 --> 00:33:12,530
Instead of saying, something is going on here, instead of being clinical and diagnosticians, they thought they had to be scientists.

230
00:33:12,530 --> 00:33:18,070
and at the end, in any social area, then you get dogmatists.

231
00:33:19,910 --> 00:33:23,850
And when things don't work, the only reaction is, let's redouble the efforts.

232
00:33:25,130 --> 00:33:33,340
And the French experience was the one, the first before modern times,

233
00:33:33,480 --> 00:33:35,920
in which economics were macroeconomics.

234
00:33:35,920 --> 00:33:45,460
And the last, the reaction to the French fiasco, which destroyed old France,

235
00:33:45,460 --> 00:33:55,080
was the shift towards microeconomics, which began with the physiocrats in 1740, 1750,

236
00:33:55,600 --> 00:33:58,640
and lasted until 1930.

237
00:34:00,520 --> 00:34:03,460
And during that time, there was a productivity crisis.

238
00:34:03,460 --> 00:34:12,720
So one can, I think, say very bluntly that experience indicates that

239
00:34:12,720 --> 00:34:20,810
while government cannot promote productivity, macroeconomics will destroy it.

240
00:34:20,810 --> 00:34:35,430
Productivity is a microeconomic event that cannot be produced by macroeconomic governmental policies,

241
00:34:35,650 --> 00:34:38,530
but can apparently be severely damaged by them.

242
00:34:40,040 --> 00:34:52,180
Which, by the way, is one reason why I do not share the prevailing euphoria about the economic policy of the present administration.

243
00:34:52,760 --> 00:34:55,920
To me, this is just another Keynesian binge.

244
00:34:56,480 --> 00:35:00,980
And very much so, with a slightly different sign in front of it.

245
00:35:01,380 --> 00:35:07,220
But with the same basic assumptions that the great white father can control and manipulate.

246
00:35:09,400 --> 00:35:13,720
And it may help a little bit if we cut the tax load and cut expenditures,

247
00:35:13,720 --> 00:35:20,780
but the root cause is the belief in, I think, a pure macroeconomic system,

248
00:35:21,240 --> 00:35:23,420
or primarily macroeconomic system.

249
00:35:23,420 --> 00:35:34,820
And that is just as built into the present administration as it was in its predecessors for the last 30 years, 25 years.

250
00:35:34,820 --> 00:35:41,820
And so we know that productivity is a local and microeconomic phenomenon.

251
00:35:41,820 --> 00:35:46,820
It is not, it can be hampered by governmental policies.

252
00:35:46,820 --> 00:35:52,980
I think it is very doubtful that it can be promoted by them or even supported.

253
00:35:53,640 --> 00:35:55,420
There's no evidence at all.

254
00:35:57,240 --> 00:36:00,000
There's lots of evidence that it can be impeded.

255
00:36:02,140 --> 00:36:09,120
But it can be made only in the local shop, in the business, in the factory,

256
00:36:10,060 --> 00:36:16,000
in, God forbid, the university, which may not like that term,

257
00:36:16,560 --> 00:36:19,690
the hospital, the police force.

258
00:36:19,690 --> 00:36:25,470
and it is something in which you have to work on all resources

259
00:36:25,470 --> 00:36:30,090
and we know it isn't working harder.

260
00:36:30,210 --> 00:36:34,270
We know that the resources require different treatment.

261
00:36:34,980 --> 00:36:38,760
Let me say money is a very peculiar resource.

262
00:36:39,580 --> 00:36:44,020
Money requires both working harder and working smarter.

263
00:36:47,020 --> 00:36:50,680
Key physical resources always require working harder

264
00:36:50,680 --> 00:36:54,100
and people always require working smarter.

265
00:36:54,100 --> 00:37:00,510
For that reason, one begins with money, because one has two approaches.

266
00:37:03,110 --> 00:37:10,030
And when you look at your receivables, that's largely a matter of working smarter.

267
00:37:11,330 --> 00:37:18,780
Do we really finance those customers whom we should finance, or are we subsidizing?

268
00:37:18,900 --> 00:37:22,660
You know, if you are a manufacturer, you can't compete with the banks.

269
00:37:24,280 --> 00:37:29,200
Their acquisition costs of capital are much too high, but they're still lower than yours.

270
00:37:30,680 --> 00:37:35,500
And so you're giving credit and saying we have no credit losses.

271
00:37:35,660 --> 00:37:36,580
It's a stupid thing.

272
00:37:38,140 --> 00:37:39,440
That's a bank's approach.

273
00:37:41,070 --> 00:37:43,130
Sure, credit losses are your restraint.

274
00:37:43,690 --> 00:37:46,030
But the question is what do you try to accomplish?

275
00:37:47,100 --> 00:37:49,320
You try to get profitable sales.

276
00:37:50,540 --> 00:37:55,360
And one looks at receivers from the point of view of a marketing strategy.

277
00:37:55,600 --> 00:38:01,030
And you'd be surprised how different it looks.

278
00:38:01,450 --> 00:38:04,230
But at the same time, one tries to make one who works harder,

279
00:38:04,230 --> 00:38:06,650
which, by the way, we have learned a good deal about.

280
00:38:07,610 --> 00:38:10,790
Now, key physical resources, one always tries to work harder.

281
00:38:11,610 --> 00:38:15,530
One tries to get more turnover per shelf space, unit of shelf space.

282
00:38:15,890 --> 00:38:19,250
One tries to get a little more yield out of a rod of copper.

283
00:38:20,480 --> 00:38:27,630
One tries to get a good deal more heating out of a BTU.

284
00:38:28,110 --> 00:38:29,350
That's working harder.

285
00:38:31,430 --> 00:38:36,350
It comes to people, especially to people whom you pay for their knowledge,

286
00:38:36,350 --> 00:38:43,310
professional people, sales people, managers, teachers.

287
00:38:44,870 --> 00:38:49,150
There one tries to make them work smarter, and that's a management duty.

288
00:38:49,730 --> 00:38:55,490
And so each resource requires its own approach, but each resource requires being worked at.

289
00:38:56,440 --> 00:38:59,460
And at the same time, you require a systems view.

290
00:39:00,170 --> 00:39:06,230
Let me give you one example of where we are grossly unproductive, for good reasons.

291
00:39:06,230 --> 00:39:13,270
we just built

292
00:39:13,270 --> 00:39:15,820
a new and very attractive

293
00:39:15,820 --> 00:39:19,110
management center which we need

294
00:39:19,110 --> 00:39:23,440
as management MBA

295
00:39:23,440 --> 00:39:27,620
and we built one of the

296
00:39:27,620 --> 00:39:29,120
nicest buildings I have seen

297
00:39:29,120 --> 00:39:35,440
for the management technology of 1870

298
00:39:35,440 --> 00:39:37,710
for the teaching technology

299
00:39:37,710 --> 00:39:39,790
I don't think our auditorium

300
00:39:39,790 --> 00:39:41,460
is there poorly

301
00:39:41,460 --> 00:39:43,560
but the rest is just

302
00:39:43,560 --> 00:39:46,450
we are going to find within five years

303
00:39:46,450 --> 00:39:48,850
that we have a lovely building that's not functional

304
00:39:48,850 --> 00:39:50,920
largely because

305
00:39:50,920 --> 00:40:09,890
There is no facilities management. The architect and the interior and so on don't work together. We are not looking at a building as something that has to be productive. It's very flexible compared to this year, you know, 20 years later.

306
00:40:09,890 --> 00:40:30,000
We learned a lot this building built 20 years ago. Even next door is already much better. We built a very inflexible building here. And facilities management, simply because up until recently the amount of money you had invested in your offices was minimal.

307
00:40:30,000 --> 00:40:52,120
And today, except that most of you will be very soon, where the physician is, who has today about between $250,000 and $600,000 in his office in capital investment. And then one doesn't just build first and look at the instrumentation secondly. Then one builds a system.

308
00:40:52,120 --> 00:41:05,020
And so facilities management is a way of integrating people and money and key physical resources in the space that is a working space.

309
00:41:05,420 --> 00:41:09,280
Factories, we have Albert Kahn started it in 1920.

310
00:41:09,940 --> 00:41:13,360
So we know a great deal, but we are not using it yet.

311
00:41:13,520 --> 00:41:17,960
But except that one has to look at all resources and then at their relationship.

312
00:41:17,960 --> 00:41:28,730
And the last thing we know about productivity is that the only things that works are unspectacular.

313
00:41:31,700 --> 00:41:39,630
The only things that works is the drop by drop that eventually hollows even the toughest stone.

314
00:41:40,470 --> 00:41:42,670
It is a continuing effort.

315
00:41:43,850 --> 00:41:49,320
And therefore, contrary to the spirit of modern management,

316
00:41:50,050 --> 00:41:56,170
when you look at our management book, and let's not personally mention any author,

317
00:41:56,170 --> 00:42:04,640
They all essentially talk about planning and setting objectives.

318
00:42:05,060 --> 00:42:13,470
They all talk about things that are major efforts that then energize a long follow-up.

319
00:42:13,890 --> 00:42:16,790
That is not the way you work on productivity.

320
00:42:18,500 --> 00:42:27,730
On productivity, you work basically far more the way the drill sergeant of old drill recruits,

321
00:42:27,730 --> 00:42:32,140
which is you do the same thing every day, but you do it.

322
00:42:34,550 --> 00:42:36,270
And you do it, and you do it.

323
00:42:36,390 --> 00:42:41,250
You don't have to profane his ears, you don't have to scream.

324
00:42:42,820 --> 00:42:48,440
Sometimes screaming, as all of you know, is both necessary and wholesome.

325
00:42:49,060 --> 00:42:52,020
But you have to do it.

326
00:42:52,480 --> 00:42:53,800
You have to set a goal.

327
00:42:54,340 --> 00:42:56,320
That real sergeant is effective

328
00:42:56,320 --> 00:43:02,510
because he knows what the finished recruit should be able to do in six weeks.

329
00:43:02,910 --> 00:43:05,250
And eight out of ten will do it.

330
00:43:05,250 --> 00:43:14,810
And he's effective because he is doing very unspectacular things steadily and faithfully.

331
00:43:16,730 --> 00:43:21,130
And so we know about productivity that we can't wait for Uncle Sam.

332
00:43:21,690 --> 00:43:23,350
That's waiting for Godot.

333
00:43:24,120 --> 00:43:31,180
And as all of you who remember that Beckett play know, the joke about Godot is he don't exist.

334
00:43:32,320 --> 00:43:36,500
And if you wait for Uncle Sam in productivity, you'll find out he don't exist.

335
00:43:38,570 --> 00:43:41,290
It's your job, my job, our job.

336
00:43:41,290 --> 00:43:54,460
day by day with a goal, with a goal of making sure that their nurses will be able to spend all their time on patient care.

337
00:43:54,640 --> 00:44:03,230
Now, that goal you will probably never attain, if only because even nurses need a coffee break, but all, but not.

338
00:44:05,390 --> 00:44:11,950
And then you can also work on their skill, because then the nurses are eager to perfect their skill.

339
00:44:11,950 --> 00:44:44,000
Today you have tremendous resistance because they say, so I'm learning something new and you so and so's again won't let me do it. They are discouraged. On your shelf space in retail, which is both the capital and the critical area, what is the goal? Is it twice the profitable?
